"1. Int J Gynaecol Obstet. 2021 Jul;154(1):44-48. doi: 10.1002/ijgo.13505. Epub\n2020  Dec 31.\n\nStorage conditions of oxytocin in a tropical climate in a low-income country.\n\nde Vries EL(1), van Tetering AAC(1), van der Hout MB(1)(2), Derijks LJJ(3), \nSseguya SP(4), Namagembe I(5), Byamugisha J(6), Oei SG(1)(2).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Department of Gynecology and Obstetrics, M\u00e1xima Medical Center, Veldhoven, \nthe Netherlands.\n(2)Department of Electrical Engineering, Eindhoven University of Technology, \nEindhoven, the Netherlands.\n(3)Department of Clinical Pharmacy and Pharmacology, M\u00e1xima Medical Center, \nVeldhoven, the Netherlands.\n(4)Department of Pharmacy, Mulago National Referral and Teaching Hospital, \nKampala, Uganda.\n(5)Directorate of Obstetrics and Gynecology, Mulago National Referral and \nTeaching Hospital, Kampala, Uganda.\n(6)Department of Gynecology and Obstetrics, School of Medicine, Makerere \nUniversity College of Health Sciences, Kampala, Uganda.\n\nOBJECTIVE: To establish the storage conditions of oxytocin in a health facility \nin a low-income country with a tropical climate, as suboptimal storage may lead \nto ineffectiveness of drugs essential to prevent and treat postpartum \nhemorrhage.\nMETHODS: At Mulago National Referral Hospital (28\u00a0000-33\u00a0000 deliveries/year) in \nKampala, Uganda, temperature logging Safe-Rx cards were placed in boxes of \noxytocin and in every known storage location. The route of the boxes through the \nhospital was tracked for 54\u00a0days, and storage conditions were observed.\nRESULTS: Oxytocin was stored within the recommended temperature range (2\u00b0C-8\u00b0C) \n24% of the time. The average temperature measured within the oxytocin boxes was \n18.2\u00b0C, with a minimum of -2.3\u00b0C and maximum of 30.4\u00b0C. Six out of twelve known \nstorage places had a refrigerator, but not one location stored medication at the \nrecommended temperature constantly. The average temperature in the storage \nplaces ranged from 9.5\u00b0C to 27.6\u00b0C, with a minimum temperature of 2.3\u00b0C and \nmaximum of 30.9\u00b0C.\nCONCLUSION: Oxytocin is not stored in the recommended temperature range for the \nmajority of time. The presence of refrigerators does not ensure adherence to \nadvised temperature storage conditions.\n\n\u00a9 2020 International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics.\n\nDOI: 10.1002/ijgo.13505\nPMID: 33251616 [Indexed for MEDLINE]"